{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Keeping a golden set honest as the bot changes</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A golden set isn't "write it once and forget it" — every real bad suggestion that reaches production is a case that was missing from the set. Adding it after the fact, with the correct expected output, is how the eval set actually grows to cover the bot's real failure modes over time instead of staying a fixed snapshot of what someone thought to test on day one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A golden set that's too small or too narrow gives false confidence — 8 cases that are all "obvious, easy" suggestions will show 100% pass rate on almost any prompt change, including a genuinely worse one, simply because none of the 8 cases exercise the part of the behavior that regressed. The set needs deliberate coverage of the ambiguous and edge cases, not just the ones the bot already handles well.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      When the scoring function itself is an LLM judge rather than a deterministic check, the judge's own prompt is part of what needs versioning and stability-checking — a judge that silently changes its own scoring criteria between runs (because the underlying judge model was upgraded, say) can show a false "quality improved" or "quality regressed" signal that has nothing to do with the suggestion bot's actual output. Pin the judge model version, and periodically spot-check judge scores against human labels on a subset, the same way you'd validate any other automated grader before trusting its verdict on a merge-blocking gate.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

